@page "/ai/scenarios/approval"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="ai/scenarios/approval"
            Title="AI tool approval"
            Keywords="human in the loop, tool approval UI, destructive tool confirmation, BitAiToolApproval, approval policy, agent safety"
            Description="A destructive tool that stops and waits for a human in a bit BlazorUI AI chat: allow once, allow always, or decline, with the turn genuinely paused while it waits." />

<DocsArticle Eyebrow="AI scenarios"
             Title="Tool approval"
             Lead="A destructive tool stops and waits for a human. Allow once, allow always, or decline."
             GitHubUrl="AI/Scenarios/Approval/AiApprovalScenarioPage.razor">

    <Badges>
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI" />
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="Scripted transport" />
    </Badges>

    <ChildContent>
        <DocSection Id="demo" Title="Live demo"
                    Description="Ask for something destructive: delete_records is declared destructive, so the session will not run it unattended.">
            <DemoExample Title="A call that waits for a decision" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example1CodeFiles" Id="example1">
                <AiScenarioFrame>
                    <BitAiChat Session="_session"
                               EmptyTitle="Ask for something destructive"
                               EmptyBody="delete_records is declared destructive, so the session will not run it unattended."
                               Suggestions="_suggestions" />
                </AiScenarioFrame>
            </DemoExample>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="how-it-works" Title="How it works">
            <p>
                Allowing once and allowing always are different decisions. Collapsing them into a single button forces the
                user either to re-approve constantly or to grant more than they meant to, so both are offered, beside a
                decline.
            </p>
            <p>
                What needs a decision is the <code>IBitAiToolApprovalPolicy</code>'s call. The default one asks for every
                tool whose <code>Risk</code> is <code>Destructive</code> (or that says <code>RequiresApproval</code>) and
                remembers the tools a user allowed always - which is why it is scoped on Blazor Server, where a singleton
                would carry one user's "always" over to every other user. <code>ai.UseApprovalPolicy&lt;T&gt;()</code>
                replaces it.
            </p>
            <p>
                While a call waits, the turn is genuinely paused: the tool has not run, the model has not been asked again,
                and stopping the turn cancels the wait. A declined call is sent back to the model as a failure it can explain.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="components" Title="Components used">
            <ul>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/toolapproval"><code>BitAiToolApproval</code></BitLink> - the prompt: allow once, allow always, or decline.</li>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/toolcallview"><code>BitAiToolCallView</code></BitLink> - the card the prompt sits in.</li>
            </ul>
        </DocSection>
    </ChildContent>

    <FooterTemplate>
        <DocPager Pages="AiScenarioNav.Items" AriaLabel="AI scenarios" />
    </FooterTemplate>
</DocsArticle>
